  "final_text": "All four steps are done.\n\n1. **Data view:** \"Kibana Sample Data Flights\" (id `d3d7af60-4c81-11e8-b3d7-01146121b73d`, index `kibana_sample_data_flights`) has 33 fields. They include `Carrier`, `Cancelled`, `Origin`, `Dest`, `AvgTicketPrice` and `timestamp`. The time field is `timestamp`.\n2. **Dashboard:** \"S4-e2416156 Flights\" (id `67690af7-f87f-41b8-a952-dab62c5ca1ac`) has two panels. One is a line chart of flights over time. The other is a bar chart of flight counts by `Carrier`, showing the top 10.\n3. **Alert rule:** \"S4-e2416156 Cancelled flights\" (id `30b767c6-a097-4712-b3cb-d5c81e352643`) is an Elasticsearch query rule. It runs every minute, counts documents with `Cancelled: true` over a 5-minute window, and fires when the count is above 10. I created it with `enabled: true`, so I didn't need to enable it afterwards.\n4. **Panel edit and read-back:** I changed the carrier panel title to \"Flights by carrier\" with a PUT to the dashboard. I had already given it that title when I created the dashboard, so the edit changed nothing. The read-back shows titles \"Flights over time\" and \"Flights by carrier\". The rule read back as `enabled: true`.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
